=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The BIND9 named daemon on srv1 (10.5.0.4) is running and loading its zone
correctly, but it has been configured with `recursion no` in
/etc/bind/named.conf. This makes it a pure authoritative-only server for the
`jeremysitlab.com` zone. The zone itself is healthy and contains a wildcard
record (`* IN A 10.5.0.4`) that would correctly answer any subdomain query
under jeremysitlab.com.

The fault manifests because every client's /etc/resolv.conf contains only
`nameserver 10.5.0.4` with no `search jeremysitlab.com` (or `domain`)
directive. Without a search domain, the OS stub resolver sends short/bare
campus hostnames — e.g. `intranet`, `srv1`, `printer` — directly to the DNS
server as root-level queries (e.g. `srv1.` at the DNS root). Because the
server is not authoritative for the root and `recursion no` prevents it from
forwarding or iterating on behalf of clients, named immediately returns
REFUSED for every such bare-name query. Users see this as "name not found."
Fully qualified names such as `srv1.jeremysitlab.com` still resolve fine.

Root cause: `recursion no` was set in srv1's /etc/bind/named.conf (noted
in its own comment as an "offline-lab" change made this morning), breaking
the server's ability to act as a campus caching/recursive resolver and
exposing the missing `search` domain on all clients.

Corrective fix: On srv1, change `recursion no` to `recursion yes` and add
`allow-recursion { any; };` in named.conf.options, then `rndc reload`. In
parallel, add `search jeremysitlab.com` to /etc/resolv.conf on every client
device — this is the most self-contained fix and works regardless of the
recursion setting, because the wildcard in the zone will match any expanded
hostname.
=== END ===
